POST /containers/{id}/attach is not exposed. It was named as a gap back when
the framed streams were undecoded; the demultiplexer (stream_frames) exists
now, so the hard part is done.

Without headers the daemon answers 200 and streams the same multiplexed
frames logs does, one-way, which the current buffering transport can handle
for a container that exits on its own.

With Upgrade: tcp + Connection: Upgrade it answers 101 Switching Protocols
and hands over a bidirectional connection - that variant needs the streaming
work in the separate ticket, and is worth splitting off rather than blocking
the one-way case on it.

Query parameters: stream, stdin, stdout, stderr, logs. There is also
/attach/ws, which needs a WebSocket handshake and is a different piece of
work again.

2026-08-27 05:15 Implemented containers->attach, one-way variant only (no Upgrade: tcp, no /attach/ws), demultiplexing through stream_frames exactly as logs does. Query parameters stream/stdin/stdout/stderr/logs; stream, stdout and stderr default to 1 because the engine defaults all five off and would return an empty body. POD says plainly that this is the 200 one-way variant, that nothing can be written to stdin here, and that it blocks until the stream ends. Coverage in t/containers_endpoints.t. What the bidirectional variant still needs is reported to the parent for its own ticket.
